{
  "abstract": "How much calibration data does a quantized vocabulary table need, and does readout calibration help when the same table supplies input embeddings? We recompressed the public Q6_K vocabulary tables of Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B with the stock Q4_K encoder. Each model scored 8,192 held-out next-token predictions from 64 English and 64 Japanese articles. On the separate 8B output head, weighting channels by their mean squared activations on 3,494 multilingual WMT states reduced dense-reference forward KL from 0.039676 to 0.020224 nats per token. Two fixed 256-state samples of uniformly drawn tokenizer IDs retained 99.8% and 99.4% of that gain. Both passed a prespecified 90% test against dense and published references. On the tied 4B table, full calibration reduced dense-reference KL from 0.064201 to 0.052124. Changing only output scoring improved every article and reduced KL by 0.012140. Changing only lookup gave a mean reduction of −0.000039, with a paired 95% interval of −0.000534 to +0.000470. The same short inputs retained 90.6% and 86.4% by point estimate on 4B, and neither established 90% retention against both references. Uniform calibrated 4B saved 36,471,328 bytes relative to a ranked mixed-precision table and missed the prespecified 0.001-nat fidelity margin. The experiments use BF16-expanded inference. The supplement provides the fitted vectors, paired article statistics and public-weight replay utilities.",
